WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.090
A train ticket reads seventy three.

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:04.920
Turn its two digit cards around and it

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:05.670
reads thirty seven.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.290
The same two digits are still here.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:10.670
Why did the value change by thirty six?

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:15.160
The seven loses nine groups of itself when it moves from

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:16.350
tens to ones.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.920
The three gains nine groups of itself on

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:19.730
the return trip.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.270
The net loss is nine times four, which is thirty six.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:25.650
Here is another ticket.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:27.410
Its digits add to eleven.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.030
Its number exceeds its reverse by twenty seven.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:33.420
Before trying numbers, what do those clues tell you

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:34.390
about the two digits?

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:41.490
Twenty seven is nine times three, so the tens digit

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:42.500
is three larger.

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.500
Set those three aside from the total of eleven.

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:47.620
The remaining eight splits equally.

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:50.040
The digits are seven and four.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:53.700
Seventy four minus forty seven really is twenty seven.

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:58.026
A three digit code exceeds its reverse by three hundred

00:00:58.026 --> 00:00:58.540
and ninety six.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:02.140
The middle digit is twice the last digit.

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:04.480
All three digits add to sixteen.

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:06.820
Which code opens the lock?

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:12.970
The middle digit stays in the tens column.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:14.870
Subtracting cancels it.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:17.560
Each unit of difference between the outside digits

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:19.410
changes the number by ninety nine.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:22.740
Three hundred and ninety six is four groups

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:23.510
of ninety nine.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.750
The first digit is four larger than the last.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:29.370
Call the last digit one part.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:31.270
The middle takes two parts.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:34.010
The first takes one part and four extra.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:35.630
Remove four from sixteen.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:39.000
Four equal parts share twelve, so the last

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.090
digit is three.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.490
The code is seven, six, three.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:46.204
Seven hundred and sixty three minus three hundred and

00:01:46.204 --> 00:01:48.090
sixty seven is three hundred and ninety six.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:51.610
The middle digit cancels in the subtraction,

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:53.770
but still counts in the digit sum.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:57.400
In a new digit puzzle, track which positions change and

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:59.390
which clues still use every digit.
